export const EW6={title:'Supporting points with evidence and concrete detail',hook:'Three supporting points are worth 45 of the letter’s 120 points—more than any other part. Judges reward “strong evidence” and “many sensory/concrete words and details.” A point that says “This bill is good for health” earns little; a point that shows a nurse checking a student’s blood sugar between classes earns much more.',objectives:['Structure each supporting point with claim, evidence and reasoning.','Use concrete, sensory details that make arguments vivid.','Draw on evidence without internet access.','Address counterarguments within supporting points.'],sections:[
['Claim, evidence, reasoning',[
'Each supporting point should be its **own paragraph** built on **claim, evidence and reasoning (CER)**. The **claim** is the topic sentence stating one reason for your position. The **evidence** supports it—facts from the topic, well-known knowledge, examples or observations. The **reasoning** explains **how** the evidence proves the claim and **why** it matters to the official’s constituents.',
'For example: **Claim:** “HB-300 would strengthen Montana’s health care workforce.” **Evidence:** “Through HOSA, students explore careers, earn certifications such as CPR and compete in health science skills.” **Reasoning:** “Investing in these students now helps fill nursing and allied health shortages in rural Montana later.”',
"Reasoning is the part writers most often skip. Evidence alone leaves the reader to make the connection; reasoning makes it for them. Ask yourself “So what?” after each piece of evidence, and write the answer as a sentence that links the evidence to the official’s constituents.",
'End each point with a sentence that ties back to your **position**, such as “For these reasons, HB-300 is an investment in Montana’s future health.”',
order('Supporting point paragraph',['Claim: topic sentence with one reason','Evidence: fact, example or observation','Concrete detail that makes it vivid','Reasoning: how it supports the claim','Link back to your position'])]],
['Concrete and sensory language',[
'The rubric repeatedly rewards **sensory and concrete words and details**. **Abstract**: “Students would benefit from more mental health support.” **Concrete**: “Right now, our counselor has more than 400 students, so a classmate who was struggling waited three weeks for an appointment.” Concrete details help the reader **see**, **hear** and **feel** the issue.',
'Use **specific nouns** (a “school nurse,” “an inhaler,” “a two-hour drive to the nearest clinic”), **strong verbs** (“collapsed,” “waited,” “trained”) and **numbers** from the topic or your experience. Replace vague words like “things,” “stuff,” “good” and “bad” with precise ones.',
'Concrete language does not mean dramatic language. Stay accurate and respectful while making the issue real.',
sort('Concrete or vague?',['Concrete','Vague'],[['“A student waited three weeks to see a counselor.”',0],['“Mental health is important.”',1],['“The nearest pediatric clinic is a two-hour drive.”',0],['“This bill will help with stuff.”',1],['“Our chapter trained 60 residents in hands-only CPR.”',0],['“Things are bad in schools.”',1]])]],
['Evidence you can use without the internet',[
'Your evidence will come from four sources. **(1) Facts in the secret topic**—amounts, who is affected and arguments on both sides. **(2) General knowledge** you have studied, stated with appropriate confidence (“Prevention is generally less costly than treatment”). **(3) Logical reasoning**—cause and effect, comparisons and consequences. **(4) Personal and community observations**—what you have seen at school, in your family, at a job or through HOSA.',
'You are not scored on factual accuracy, but **credibility** still matters. Do not invent precise statistics or quote experts you cannot name. Use qualified phrases—“research suggests,” “health experts generally agree,” “in my experience”—when you are not certain.',
'Combining types makes a point persuasive: a fact from the topic, a logical consequence and a concrete example from your community.',
bench('Is the evidence used credibly?',[{text:'Invented letter sentence: “According to the bill summary, SB-12 would fund one full-time nurse for each of our state’s 410 public schools.”',claim:'This sentence uses evidence from the secret topic.',supported:true,why:'It cites the topic’s bill summary as its source.'},{text:'Invented letter sentence: “Exactly 87.3% of all students in America have asthma.”',claim:'This sentence is a credible use of evidence without research.',supported:false,why:'An invented precise statistic hurts credibility, and it is implausible.'},{text:'Invented letter sentence: “In my experience as a HOSA member, students who learn CPR feel more confident responding to emergencies.”',claim:'This sentence uses personal observation with appropriate qualification.',supported:true,why:'It is honest about its source and scope.'},{text:'Invented letter sentence: “Everyone knows this bill will save a billion dollars.”',claim:'This sentence provides strong, credible evidence.',supported:false,why:'It is vague and overstated with no source.'}])]],
['Making the three points distinct',[
'Each point should cover a **different reason**. Plan them around different argument types: **health** (outcomes, safety, prevention), **economic** (costs, savings, return on investment, jobs), **equity** (access, fairness, rural or underserved communities), **education and workforce** (skills, careers, attendance) or **practicality** (feasibility, enforcement, existing models).',
'Order your points strategically: lead with a **strong** point, put your **most compelling** point last so it stays with the reader, and place a solid supporting point in the middle. Use **transitions** to signal each new point: “First,” “In addition,” “Most importantly,” “Furthermore.”',
'If two points overlap, combine them and find a new third reason.',
"A quick test: cover the topic sentences of your three paragraphs and read only the evidence. If two paragraphs could swap evidence without changing anything, the points are too similar. Distinct points each need their own evidence and their own reasoning.",
match('Point and argument type',[['Fewer emergency visits for asthma','Health'],['Long-term savings from prevention','Economic'],['Rural students gain access','Equity'],['Students prepared for health careers','Workforce'],['Similar programs work in other states','Practicality'],['Students miss less class','Education']])]],
['Addressing counterarguments',[
'Acknowledging the other side shows the official you have considered the issue fully. Use a **concession and rebuttal**: “Some argue that the bill is too costly. However, $500,000 shared among seven organizations is a modest investment compared with the cost of workforce shortages.” Place the rebuttal inside a supporting point or in a short paragraph before your conclusion.',
'Respond to the **strongest** opposing argument in the topic—not a weak one. Keep the tone respectful: avoid calling opponents wrong or foolish. Focus on evidence and reasoning.',
'A good rebuttal can turn an opponent’s concern into support for your view, such as answering “evidence of effectiveness is lacking” with specific outcomes you have seen.',
chk('Which is the best rebuttal sentence?',['“While some worry about cost, the bill’s modest $500,000 investment reaches thousands of students and strengthens our future workforce.”','“Opponents are wrong and do not care about students.”','“I ignore the other side.”','“Cost does not matter at all.”'],'Concede respectfully, then answer with evidence.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Strengthening a point',{a:{text:'A draft point reads: “Second, school nurses are good for health.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is missing?',choices:[['Specific evidence, concrete detail and reasoning','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing; it is complete','b','It is only a vague claim.',false]]},b:{text:'Which revision is strongest?',choices:[['“Second, a full-time nurse can manage students’ chronic conditions. At my school, a nurse checks a classmate’s blood sugar before lunch each day, which lets him stay in class instead of going home. SB-12 would give every student that safety net.”','c','Correct. Claim, evidence, detail and reasoning.',true],['“Second, nurses are really, really good.”','c','Intensifiers are not evidence.',false]]},c:{text:'You add a transition and end the paragraph by linking back to your position.',end:true}})]],
["Transitions and paragraph flow",["Transitions guide the reader from one point to the next. Use **ordering transitions** (“First,” “Second,” “Finally”), **adding transitions** (“In addition,” “Furthermore,” “Moreover”), **emphasis transitions** (“Most importantly,” “Above all”), **contrast transitions** (“However,” “Although,” “While some argue”) and **cause-and-effect transitions** (“As a result,” “Therefore,” “Because of this”).","Each body paragraph should begin with a transition and topic sentence and end with a sentence that ties back to your position. This structure makes your letter easy to skim—important for busy legislative staff who may read dozens of letters a day.","Vary transitions so the letter does not sound repetitive, and keep paragraphs to about four to six sentences.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Transition and purpose","pairs":[["First","Ordering"],["In addition","Adding a point"],["Most importantly","Emphasis"],["However","Contrast"],["As a result","Cause and effect"],["For these reasons","Concluding"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Three supporting points are worth 45 points; build each paragraph with a claim, evidence and reasoning, end by linking to your position and use transitions.','Use concrete, sensory details—specific nouns, strong verbs and numbers from the topic or your experience—instead of vague words; stay accurate and respectful.','Draw evidence from the topic, general knowledge, logic and personal observation; never invent precise statistics; make the three points different types (health, economic, equity, workforce, practicality); concede and rebut the strongest counterargument respectfully.'],terms:[['Claim','A statement of one reason supporting your position.'],['Concrete detail','A specific, sensory detail that helps the reader picture the issue.'],['Rebuttal','A response that answers an opposing argument.']],mcqs:[q('How many points are the three supporting points worth together?','45','15','30','60','15 each.'),q('What does CER stand for?','Claim, evidence, reasoning','Cause, effect, result','Content, emotion, review','Count, edit, revise','Paragraph structure.'),q('Which sentence uses concrete detail?','“The nearest pediatric clinic is a two-hour drive.”','“Health is important.”','“Things are bad.”','“This bill helps stuff.”','Specific and vivid.'),q('Which evidence source is NOT appropriate during the event?','Internet research','Facts in the secret topic','Personal observation','General knowledge','Research is not allowed.'),q('Where should your most compelling point usually go?','Last','First only','Nowhere','In the subject line','Stays with the reader.'),q('Which is a respectful rebuttal?','“While some worry about cost, the investment is modest compared with the benefits.”','“Opponents are foolish.”','“The other side is lying.”','Ignoring the objection','Concede, then answer.'),q('Why avoid invented precise statistics?','They damage credibility','They earn bonus points','Judges require them','They are always accurate','Honesty matters.'),q('If two of your points overlap, you should:','Combine them and find a new third reason','Keep both anyway','Delete all points','Write the same point three times','Distinct points.')],frq:{prompt:'Write one complete supporting-point paragraph for a health policy letter (invented details are fine) and explain how it uses claim, evidence, reasoning and concrete detail.',rubric:[['Claim',['claim','topic sentence','reason','first','second']],['Evidence',['topic','fact','example','observation','experience']],['Concrete detail',['concrete','specific','sensory','numbers','vivid']],['Reasoning and link',['reasoning','because','constituents','link','position','transition']]],model:'Supporting paragraph: “Most importantly, SB-12 would help students with chronic illnesses stay safely in class. At my high school, one nurse serves three buildings, so a classmate with type 1 diabetes often has to leave math class and walk across campus to the office, where a secretary calls his mother to drive in from work whenever his blood sugar drops. With a full-time nurse in every school, as SB-12 provides, students like him could have their glucose checked and treated within minutes, without missing instruction or putting a parent’s job at risk. Keeping students healthy and in class is exactly the kind of practical, family-centered investment our community needs, and it is why I urge you to support SB-12.” Explanation: The paragraph opens with a transition and a clear claim—SB-12 would help students with chronic illnesses stay in class. The evidence comes from the secret topic’s description of the bill and from my own observation of how one nurse is shared among buildings. Concrete details—a classmate with type 1 diabetes, leaving math class, walking across campus, a parent driving in from work—help the reader picture the problem, and they replace vague claims like “nurses are good.” The reasoning explains how full-time nurses would solve this problem and why it matters to families and jobs in the official’s district, and the final sentence links back to my position and the action I am requesting.'},sources:[RULES,OWL_ARG]};

export const EW7={title:'The personal story and its significance',hook:'Legislators hear statistics all day. What they remember is a constituent who explains how a policy changed—or could change—a real life. The personal story section is worth 15 points and asks you to show the topic’s significance to you and your community. Done well, it turns your letter from an essay into a voice the official cannot ignore.',objectives:['Explain what the rubric means by personal story and significance.','Choose a story that connects directly to the policy.','Use narrative techniques to make the story vivid and brief.','Connect personal experience to community impact while protecting privacy.'],sections:[
['What the rubric rewards',[
'The **personal story** criterion (15 points) rewards a writer who **demonstrates command of the topic**, **discusses its significance** to the competitor and/or community, **uses the topic as a path** for the letter and offers **explanations and insights** that enhance understanding. Lower scores go to letters that only mention the topic briefly without analysis of why it matters.',
'This is your chance to show **why you care**. A story about your own experience, your family, your friends, your school or your community—told briefly and connected clearly to the bill—shows the official the human impact of their decision.',
'The story should not be a separate tangent. It must **illustrate** your position and lead naturally back to the policy.',
chk('What does the personal story criterion reward most?',['Explaining the topic’s significance to you and your community with insight','The longest possible story','A story unrelated to the bill','Listing statistics only'],'Connect your experience to the policy and its impact.')]],
['Choosing the right story',[
'Choose a story that is **directly connected** to the policy. For a school nurse bill: a classmate’s allergic reaction or a family member’s chronic illness. For a CTSO funding bill: how HOSA helped you discover a career, gain a skill or serve your community. For a tobacco bill: watching friends start vaping or a relative’s struggle with smoking-related illness.',
'If you do not have a direct experience, use your **community**: what you have observed at school, in your town or through HOSA service. You can also describe **why the issue matters** to people you represent as a student—such as classmates who would benefit.',
'Stay **truthful**. A modest, honest connection is more persuasive than a dramatic story that sounds invented.',
"Before the event, list four or five real experiences you could adapt: a time HOSA taught you a skill, a family member’s health care experience, something you observed at school, a volunteer activity or a challenge in your community. Under time pressure, you can pick the best fit instead of searching your memory.",
match('Policy and fitting story',[['School nurse funding','A classmate’s allergic reaction at school'],['CTSO funding','How HOSA led you to a nursing career goal'],['Flavored vape ban','Watching friends become dependent on nicotine'],['Rural clinic funding','A family’s long drive for a child’s checkup'],['Mental health counselors','A friend waiting weeks for help'],['Naloxone access','A community overdose prevention training']])]],
['Telling it vividly and briefly',[
'Keep the story to **one paragraph**, about **four to six sentences**. Use a mini-narrative structure: **set the scene** (when and where), describe **what happened** with concrete details, show **how it affected** you or others and explain **what it taught you** about the policy.',
'Use **sensory and concrete details**—what you saw, heard or felt—but stay focused. One vivid moment is better than a long timeline. Write in **first person** (“I,” “my”) and in a sincere, respectful tone.',
'End by **connecting back**: “That afternoon showed me why HB-300 matters: without HOSA, I would never have known how to help.”',
order('Mini-narrative structure',['Set the scene','Describe what happened with a concrete detail','Show the effect on you or others','State what it taught you','Connect back to the bill'])]],
['From personal to community significance',[
'After your story, broaden it: explain how **many people** in the official’s district face similar situations and what the policy would change for them. This moves your letter from one experience to **community impact**—the official’s responsibility.',
'For example: “My experience is not unusual. In rural counties across our state, families drive hours for basic care. SB-30 would bring clinics closer to thousands of families like mine.” Use facts from the topic to support the broader claim.',
'Insights earn top scores: explain **why** the problem exists or **how** the policy solves it, not just that it matters.',
"A useful sentence pattern is: “My experience reflects a larger problem: [problem]. [Bill] would help by [mechanism], so that families like mine can [outcome].” This links your story, the community and the policy in one sentence.",
sort('Personal only or connected to community?',['Personal only','Connected to community impact'],[['“I like HOSA a lot.”',0],['“HOSA gave me CPR skills, and our chapter has trained 60 residents—imagine that across every Montana chapter.”',1],['“My cousin vapes.”',0],['“Like my cousin, many teens in our county started vaping with flavored products, which this ban would remove from store shelves.”',1]])]],
['Privacy and respect',[
'Protect others’ **privacy**. Do not include full names, specific medical details or identifying information about friends or family without permission. Use general terms: “a classmate,” “a family member,” “a friend from my soccer team.”',
'Be **respectful**: avoid blaming individuals, exaggerating suffering or using sensitive stories for shock value. If a story is very personal or painful, choose how much to share; you can describe the impact without revealing everything.',
'Your goal is to help the official understand the human side of the policy while treating everyone in the story with dignity.',
sort('Respectful or not?',['Respectful','Not respectful'],[['“A classmate with a severe peanut allergy…”',0],['Using a friend’s full name and diagnosis without permission',1],['Describing the impact on your family honestly',0],['Exaggerating a story for shock value',1],['“A family member who struggled with addiction…”',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Adding a story',{a:{text:'Your letter supports a bill funding school mental health counselors, but you have never needed counseling yourself. (Scenario invented for practice.) What story can you tell?',choices:[['What you have observed: friends waiting weeks for help and how it affected them and your school','b','Correct. Community observation counts.',true],['Invent a dramatic personal crisis','b','Stay truthful.',false]]},b:{text:'How should you describe the friends?',choices:[['Generally—“several friends”—without names or details they would not want shared','c','Correct.',true],['With full names and diagnoses','c','Protect privacy.',false]]},c:{text:'You end the paragraph by explaining how more counselors would help many students in the district.',end:true}})]],
["Where to place the story",["You can place the personal story in several ways. **Option 1: its own paragraph** after the three supporting points, before the conclusion—clear and easy for judges to find. **Option 2: a brief hook** in the opening, with the full story later. **Option 3: woven into a supporting point**, where it serves as evidence. Many strong letters use a short hook in the opening and a full story paragraph near the end.","Whichever option you choose, make the story **easy to identify** and clearly connected to significance for you and your community. Judges score it as a separate 15-point criterion, so do not let it disappear inside another paragraph.","Practice telling your most likely stories—about HOSA, your school, your family’s health experiences or your community—so you can adapt them quickly to many topics.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Clear story placement?","buckets":["Clear","Unclear"],"items":[["A full story paragraph before the conclusion",0],["A one-sentence hook in the opening plus a later story paragraph",0],["A story buried in a single clause",1],["A story unrelated to the bill",1],["A story woven into point 3 and linked to community impact",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['The personal story (15 points) rewards command of the topic and explanation of its significance to you and your community, using the topic as a path with insight—not a tangent.','Choose a truthful story directly connected to the policy (your own, your family’s or what you observe in your community); tell it in one 4–6 sentence paragraph: scene, concrete detail, effect, lesson, link back.','Broaden from personal to community impact with facts from the topic and insight into why the problem exists and how the policy helps; protect privacy with general terms and treat everyone with respect.'],terms:[['Personal story','A brief real experience that shows why a policy matters.'],['Significance','Why an issue matters to a person or community.'],['First person','Writing from the writer’s own point of view using “I” and “my.”']],mcqs:[q('How many points is the personal story worth?','15','5','10','45','Content section.'),q('How long should the personal story usually be?','One paragraph of about 4–6 sentences','Two pages','One word','The whole letter','Brief and vivid.'),q('What if you have no direct personal experience?','Use what you have observed in your community','Invent a story','Skip the section','Copy a story from a website','Truthful observation.'),q('How should you refer to a friend in your story?','Generally, such as “a classmate”','By full name and diagnosis','By their social media handle','By their address','Privacy.'),q('What should the story end with?','A connection back to the bill','A new topic','A joke','Nothing','Use the topic as a path.'),q('Which shows community significance?','“Many families in our county face the same long drive.”','“I like this bill.”','“My cousin is nice.”','“That is all.”','Broaden the impact.'),q('Which point of view is used for a personal story?','First person','Third person only','Second person only','No point of view','“I” and “my.”'),q('Why avoid exaggerating a story?','It hurts credibility and respect','It earns more points','Judges require it','It is more truthful','Honesty persuades.')],frq:{prompt:'Write a personal story paragraph for a letter supporting a health policy (invented details are fine) and explain how it shows significance to you and your community.',rubric:[['Story',['scene','happened','concrete','felt','learned','first person']],['Connection',['bill','policy','connect','why it matters','path']],['Community significance',['many','district','community','families','students','insight']],['Respect and privacy',['classmate','family member','privacy','truthful','respectful']]],model:'Story paragraph: “Last spring, at a HOSA community health fair, I helped a nurse volunteer take blood pressure readings in our town library. An older man sitting at my table had a reading so high that the nurse urged him to see a doctor that day; he admitted he had not had a checkup in years because the nearest clinic was more than an hour away and he no longer drove at night. Watching him leave with a referral, I realized that our chapter’s small event may have prevented a stroke. That afternoon taught me that HOSA does more than prepare students for careers—it brings health services to people who would otherwise go without. HB-300 would allow chapters like mine to hold more of these events across Montana.” Explanation: The paragraph sets the scene, describes what happened with concrete details—the library table, the high reading, the hour-long drive—and shows its effect on me and on the man. It explains what I learned and connects directly back to HB-300, using the topic as a path rather than a tangent. It moves from my personal experience to community significance by suggesting that many rural residents face similar barriers and that funding would let chapters reach more of them, which is insight the official can act on. To protect privacy, the story refers to “an older man” without any name or identifying details, and it stays truthful and respectful rather than dramatic.'},sources:[RULES,APHA_ADV]};

export const EW8={title:'The conclusion, request for response and signature block',hook:'The final paragraph is the last thing the official reads before deciding whether to remember your letter. It is worth 15 points: 10 for a concise conclusion that restates your position in a new way and leaves the reader thinking, and 5—all-or-nothing—for clearly asking for a response. Then comes the signature block, which earns another 5.',objectives:['Write a concise conclusion that summarizes points and restates the position in a new way.','Request a response clearly and politely.','Format the closing, signature and HOSA identifying information correctly.','Avoid common conclusion mistakes.'],sections:[
['What a strong conclusion does',[
'The rubric’s top conclusion is **concise**, **summarizes the supporting points**, **restates the position in a new way** and leaves the judge **satisfied** and **with something to think about**. A weak conclusion repeats the opening word for word, introduces new arguments or ends abruptly.',
'A strong conclusion usually has **three to five sentences**: **(1)** a restatement of your position in fresh words; **(2)** a brief summary of your three points; **(3)** a forward-looking or memorable final thought; and **(4)** a **request for a response**.',
'Use a transition such as “In conclusion,” “For these reasons” or “Ultimately” to signal the ending.',
"Keep the conclusion proportional: if your letter is about one page, the conclusion should be a short paragraph, not a second body. Officials and judges should be able to read it in under thirty seconds and know exactly what you want them to do.",
order('Conclusion structure',['Transition into the conclusion','Restate your position in new words','Summarize your three points briefly','End with a memorable final thought','Request a response'])]],
['Restating in a new way',[
'Restating “in a new way” means expressing the same position with **different words** and a **broader perspective**. Opening: “I urge you to vote yes on HB-300.” Conclusion: “By supporting HB-300, you can invest in thousands of Montana students who are ready to become the nurses, paramedics and physicians our communities need.”',
'Connect the conclusion to **values** the official cares about—healthy communities, wise use of public funds, opportunity for young people, safety. A memorable final thought might look ahead: “A vote for HB-300 today is a vote for healthier Montana communities tomorrow.”',
'Do **not** add a new argument in the conclusion. If you think of a new point late, fold it into a body paragraph instead.',
sort('Strong or weak conclusion sentence?',['Strong','Weak'],[['“By supporting SB-12, you can make every school a safer place to learn.”',0],['“That’s all I have to say.”',1],['“For these reasons—safety, attendance and family stability—I urge your support.”',0],['“Also, here is a fourth reason I forgot earlier.”',1],['“A vote for HB-300 today is a vote for healthier communities tomorrow.”',0]])]],
['Requesting a response',[
'The **request for a response** is worth **5 points** and is **all-or-nothing**: the competitor must **clearly** request a response. Use a direct sentence: “I would appreciate a response letting me know your position on HB-300.” or “Please reply to let me know how you plan to vote.” or “I look forward to hearing from you about this important issue.”',
'Make it easy to respond by referring to your contact information: “You can reach me at the email address above.” Place the request in the **final paragraph** so it is the last action you ask for.',
'A vague closing like “Thank you for your time” is polite but **does not** count as a request for a response. Include both a thank-you and an explicit request.',
chk('Which sentence clearly requests a response?',['“I would appreciate a reply letting me know your position on HB-300.”','“Thank you for your time.”','“Have a nice day.”','“Sincerely,”'],'The request must be explicit.')]],
['The closing and signature block',[
'After the final paragraph, use a professional **complimentary close** such as “**Sincerely,**” or “**Respectfully,**” followed by your **typed name**. Then add the required **competitor identifying information**: **name, HOSA division, chapter number, school name and chartered association**. This item is worth **5 points**; providing only three of the five earns partial credit and one or two earns little.',
'Example: “Jordan Rivera, Secondary Division, HOSA Chapter #000, Cricket High School, Montana HOSA.” Use the real information for your chapter at competition. Double-check spelling of your school and association.',
'Remember that your **constituent mailing address and email** belong in the constituent block (usually at the top). Both blocks are required.',
flip('Signature block items',[['Complimentary close','Sincerely, or Respectfully,'],['Typed name','Your full name'],['HOSA division','Secondary or Postsecondary/Collegiate'],['Chapter number','Your HOSA chapter #'],['School name','Your school'],['Chartered association','Your state HOSA association']])]],
['A model conclusion',[
ex('Conclusion for the sample topic','Write a concluding paragraph supporting HB-300 (details invented for practice).',['Transition and new restatement: “For these reasons, I believe HB-300 is one of the wisest investments Montana can make in its future.”','Summary of points: “Funding CTSOs like HOSA builds our health workforce, expands student service in our communities and delivers strong value for a modest cost.”','Memorable thought: “The students you support today will be the nurses and paramedics caring for Montanans tomorrow.”','Request: “I would be grateful for a reply letting me know your position on HB-300. Thank you for your time and service.”','Close: “Sincerely, / Jordan Rivera / Jordan Rivera, Secondary Division, HOSA Chapter #000, Cricket High School, Montana HOSA”'],'The conclusion restates the position in new words, summarizes three points, leaves a final thought and clearly requests a response (example invented for practice).'),
'Practice writing conclusions for several topics in under five minutes. Because the request for a response and the signature block are all-or-nothing points, make them part of your memorized template.']],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Checking the ending',{a:{text:'Your conclusion ends: “Thank you for your time. Sincerely, Maya Chen.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is missing?',choices:[['An explicit request for a response and the full HOSA identifying information','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing','b','Both items are required for points.',false]]},b:{text:'You add: “Please let me know your position.” What else completes the signature block?',choices:[['HOSA division, chapter number, school name and chartered association','c','Correct.',true],['Your phone number only','c','The rubric lists five identifying items.',false]]},c:{text:'Your ending now earns all conclusion and identification points.',end:true}})]],
["Common conclusion mistakes",["The most frequent conclusion errors are **ending abruptly** without a closing paragraph, **repeating the opening word for word**, **introducing a new argument**, **forgetting the request for a response** and **switching or softening the position** (“Maybe you could consider it”).","Another mistake is running out of time. Because the conclusion and request are worth 15 points, write a short, complete conclusion before polishing body paragraphs. You can always improve it during revision.","A quick check: Does the final paragraph restate the position in new words, summarize the three points, leave a final thought and explicitly ask for a reply?",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Conclusion mistake or best practice?","buckets":["Mistake","Best practice"],"items":[["Ending with “That’s it.”",0],["Restating the position in fresh words",1],["Adding a brand-new argument",0],["Explicitly asking for a reply",1],["“Maybe you could think about it.”",0],["Summarizing all three points briefly",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['The conclusion (10 points) is concise, summarizes the three points, restates the position in a new way and leaves a memorable final thought; do not add new arguments.','The request for a response (5 points, all-or-nothing) must be explicit—“I would appreciate a reply letting me know your position”—not just “Thank you for your time.”','Close with “Sincerely,” and your name, then your name, HOSA division, chapter number, school and chartered association (5 points); keep constituent address and email in the constituent block.'],terms:[['Conclusion','The final paragraph that restates the position and summarizes the argument.'],['Request for response','An explicit sentence asking the official to reply.'],['Complimentary close','A professional sign-off such as “Sincerely,”.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is the conclusion worth?','10','5','15','20','Plus 5 for the request.'),q('Is the request for a response all-or-nothing?','Yes','No','Only at ILC','Only for postsecondary','5 points.'),q('Which sentence counts as a request for a response?','“Please reply to let me know how you plan to vote.”','“Thank you for your time.”','“Have a great day.”','“Sincerely,”','Explicit.'),q('What should the conclusion NOT include?','A new argument','A restatement','A summary of points','A final thought','Fold new points into the body.'),q('Which item is part of the competitor identifying information?','Chapter number','Favorite subject','Locker number','Shoe size','Five items.'),q('Restating “in a new way” means:','Expressing the position with different words and a broader perspective','Copying the opening word for word','Changing your position','Leaving it out','Fresh wording.'),q('Which complimentary close is professional?','“Sincerely,”','“Later,”','“Peace out,”','“Bye!”','Formal.'),q('Where do your mailing address and email belong?','In the constituent block','In the subject line','In the conclusion only','Nowhere','Required item.')],frq:{prompt:'Write a concluding paragraph and signature block for a health policy letter (invented details are fine), and explain how each part earns its rubric points.',rubric:[['Conclusion',['restate','new way','summarize','three points','final thought','concise']],['Request',['request','reply','response','position','explicit']],['Signature',['sincerely','name','division','chapter','school','chartered association']],['Explanation',['10 points','5 points','all-or-nothing','no new argument']]],model:'Conclusion: “Ultimately, SB-12 is about making sure no student’s safety depends on which building their school nurse happens to be in that day. A full-time nurse in every school would protect students in emergencies, help those with chronic illnesses stay in class and relieve teachers and working parents of medical duties they are not trained to handle. The students you protect today will be healthier, more successful members of our community tomorrow. I would appreciate a reply letting me know your position on SB-12, and I thank you for your time and service. Sincerely, Maya Chen. Maya Chen, Secondary Division, HOSA Chapter #000, Lincoln High School, Ohio HOSA.” Explanation: The paragraph begins with a transition and restates my support in a new way, focusing on the broader idea of equal safety for every student rather than repeating my opening sentence. It briefly summarizes my three points—emergencies, chronic illness and relief for teachers and parents—and ends with a forward-looking thought that leaves the reader with something to consider, earning the 10 conclusion points without adding any new argument. The explicit sentence asking for a reply about my position earns the all-or-nothing 5 points for requesting a response, and the thank-you keeps the tone respectful. Finally, the signature block includes a professional close, my name and all five identifying items—name, HOSA division, chapter number, school name and chartered association—for the competitor information points.'},sources:[RULES,OWL_LETTER]};

export const EW9={title:'Grammar, spelling, punctuation and professional tone',hook:'Ten points ride on mechanics: five for grammar and five for spelling and punctuation, with full credit only for zero errors. Just three or four mistakes drop each score to average. Under time pressure, small errors multiply—so knowing the most common ones and saving time to proofread can be the difference between first and fifth place.',objectives:['Avoid the most common grammar errors in persuasive letters.','Spell common policy and health terms correctly.','Use commas, apostrophes and other punctuation correctly.','Write in a formal, respectful and concise tone.'],sections:[
['How mechanics are scored',[
'**Grammar** (5 points): **zero** errors earns 5; **1–2** errors earns 4; **3–4** earns 3; **5–6** earns 2; **more than 6** earns 0. **Spelling and punctuation** (5 points) uses the same scale. Because errors are counted, a long letter with many small mistakes can lose more points than a shorter, carefully proofread one.',
'Reserve at least **eight minutes** at the end to proofread. Use your typing program’s **spell check and grammar check**, but do not rely on them completely—they miss errors like “their” versus “there” and may suggest wrong corrections.',
'Read your letter **slowly**, ideally from the end to the beginning one sentence at a time, to catch errors your eyes skip when reading for meaning.',
"Another strategy is to read the letter in a different view—for example, by temporarily enlarging the font or changing its color. A fresh look helps your eyes notice errors. If your device has a read-aloud feature and you can use it silently with headphones, hearing your words can reveal missing or repeated words.",
{t:'table',caption:'Mechanics scoring scale',head:['Errors','Grammar points','Spelling and punctuation points'],rows:[['0','5','5'],['1–2','4','4'],['3–4','3','3'],['5–6','2','2'],['More than 6','0','0']]}]],
['Common grammar errors',[
'**Subject–verb agreement**: “The bill **provides**,” “The students **need**,” “Each of the schools **has**.” **Pronoun agreement**: “Every student deserves **their**…” is widely accepted, but be consistent. **Sentence fragments**: “Because nurses save lives.” needs a main clause. **Run-on sentences and comma splices**: “The bill is important, it helps students” should use a period, semicolon or conjunction.',
'**Verb tense**: stay consistent—describe the bill in the present (“HB-300 would provide”) and your experiences in the past (“I joined HOSA”). **Commonly confused words**: **its** (possessive) vs **it’s** (it is); **their/there/they’re**; **affect** (verb) vs **effect** (usually noun); **then** vs **than**; **who’s** vs **whose**.',
'Use **active voice** for clarity: “Nurses manage chronic conditions” is stronger than “Chronic conditions are managed by nurses.”',
"Also watch for **unclear pronouns**. In “The senator met the students, and they agreed,” it is not clear who agreed. Replace vague pronouns with nouns when there could be confusion, especially in a letter that refers to the official, the bill and several groups of people.",
match('Error and correction',[['“The bill provide funding”','“The bill provides funding”'],['“Its important to act”','“It’s important to act”'],['“Their are many students”','“There are many students”'],['“The bill will effect families”','“The bill will affect families”'],['“The bill is vital, it helps students”','“The bill is vital; it helps students”'],['“Because nurses save lives.”','“I support SB-12 because nurses save lives.”']])]],
['Spelling policy and health words',[
'Some words appear often in policy letters and are easy to misspell under pressure: **legislature**, **legislation**, **constituent**, **representative**, **government**, **appropriation**, **necessary**, **recommend**, **sincerely**, **separate**, **accommodate**, **committee**, **privilege**, **receive** and **definitely**. Health terms such as **diabetes**, **asthma**, **anaphylaxis** and **psychiatrist** also appear.',
'Double-check **proper nouns**: the official’s name, the bill title, your school and your chartered association. Copy them carefully from the topic and your memory.',
'Practice spelling these words until they are automatic.',
spell('Spell common policy-letter words',[['constituent','A person represented by an elected official'],['legislature','The body that makes state laws'],['appropriation','Money set aside by a legislature for a purpose'],['committee','A group of legislators that reviews bills'],['sincerely','A common letter closing'],['necessary','Required or needed'],['recommend','To suggest as best'],['accommodate','To provide for or make room for']])]],
['Punctuation essentials',[
'**Commas**: use them after introductory phrases (“As a constituent, I…”), between items in a series (“safety, attendance and family stability”—HOSA style varies; be consistent), before a conjunction joining two independent clauses (“The bill is modest, but its impact is large”) and around nonessential phrases (“HOSA, a student organization, …”). Do **not** put a comma between a subject and its verb.',
'**Apostrophes** show possession (“the state’s budget,” “students’ needs”) and contractions—but in formal letters, **avoid contractions** (“do not” instead of “don’t”). **Colons** follow formal salutations (“Dear Senator Wilson:”) and introduce lists. Use **quotation marks** only for direct quotes and **capitalize** titles before names (“Senator Wilson”).',
'Avoid **exclamation points** in formal policy letters; let your words carry the emphasis.',
sort('Correct or incorrect punctuation?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['“As a constituent, I urge your support.”',0],['“The students, need more nurses.”',1],['“the state’s budget”',0],['“the states budget”',1],['“Dear Senator Wilson:”',0],['“This bill is AMAZING!!!”',1]])]],
['Professional tone and concision',[
'Write **formally** and **respectfully**: no slang, text abbreviations (“u,” “b/c”), emojis or sarcasm. Address the official as a partner in solving a problem, even if you disagree with them. Avoid absolute claims (“everyone,” “never”) unless supported.',
'Be **concise**: replace wordy phrases with simple ones (“in order to” → “to”; “due to the fact that” → “because”; “at this point in time” → “now”). Plain language guidelines recommend short sentences, common words and active voice. A focused letter of about one page is more persuasive than a long one.',
'Read your letter as the official would: Is it clear, respectful and easy to act on?',
sort('Professional or unprofessional?',['Professional','Unprofessional'],[['“I respectfully urge you to support HB-300.”',0],['“u should def vote yes lol”',1],['“because”',0],['“due to the fact that”',1],['“I understand your concerns about cost.”',0],['“Only a fool would vote no.”',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Proofreading pass',{a:{text:'With eight minutes left, you notice “The bill will effect thousands of familys.” (Scenario invented for practice.) How many errors?',choices:[['Two: “effect” should be “affect” and “familys” should be “families”','b','Correct.',true],['None','b','Check confused words and plurals.',false]]},b:{text:'You also find “Its important to fund nurses, they help students.” What fixes it?',choices:[['“It’s important to fund nurses because they help students.”','c','Correct. Fixes the contraction and the comma splice.',true],['Leave it—spell check did not flag it','c','Spell check misses many errors.',false]]},c:{text:'You read the letter backward sentence by sentence and fix two more small errors before saving.',end:true}})]],
["Formal letter conventions",["**Titles and names**: capitalize titles before names (“Senator Wilson,” “Governor Ortiz”) and use the official’s last name in the salutation. **Dates**: write them out (“March 3, 2027”). **Numbers**: spell out one through nine and use numerals for 10 and above, except for money and percentages (“$500,000,” “40 percent” or “40%”—be consistent). **Bill numbers**: copy them exactly (“HB-300,” “SB 12”).","**Paragraphs**: in block format, separate paragraphs with a blank line rather than indenting. **Abbreviations**: spell out terms on first use—“Career and Technology Student Organizations (CTSOs)”—then use the abbreviation.","**Font and length**: use a standard font such as Arial or Times New Roman in 11–12 point, and aim for about one page. Consistency in these small choices makes the letter look polished and professional.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Convention and example","pairs":[["Title before a name","Senator Wilson"],["Date","March 3, 2027"],["Small number","three schools"],["Money","$500,000"],["First use of an abbreviation","Career and Technology Student Organizations (CTSOs)"],["Paragraph spacing","Blank line between paragraphs"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Grammar and spelling/punctuation are 5 points each: 0 errors = 5, 1–2 = 4, 3–4 = 3, 5–6 = 2, more than 6 = 0; reserve at least eight minutes to proofread and do not rely only on spell check.','Watch subject–verb agreement, fragments, run-ons and comma splices, consistent tense and confused words (its/it’s, their/there/they’re, affect/effect, then/than); spell constituent, legislature, appropriation, committee, sincerely and necessary correctly.','Use commas after introductory phrases and before conjunctions joining clauses, apostrophes for possession, a colon after formal salutations and no exclamation points or contractions; write formally, respectfully and concisely in active voice.'],terms:[['Comma splice','Two independent clauses joined only by a comma.'],['Sentence fragment','An incomplete sentence missing a main clause.'],['Active voice','Writing in which the subject performs the action.']],mcqs:[q('How many errors earn full grammar points?','Zero','One','Two','Three','Error-free.'),q('Which is correct?','“It’s important to act.”','“Its important to act.”','“Its’ important to act.”','“It is’ important.”','It is.'),q('Which word is spelled correctly?','Constituent','Constituant','Constitutent','Consituent','Represented person.'),q('Which sentence has a comma splice?','“The bill is vital, it helps students.”','“The bill is vital; it helps students.”','“The bill is vital because it helps students.”','“The bill is vital.”','Two clauses with only a comma.'),q('Which sentence uses affect or effect correctly?','“The bill will affect families.”','“The bill will effect families.”','“The bill will affects families.”','“The bill effect families.”','Affect is the verb.'),q('Which punctuation follows a formal salutation?','A colon','An exclamation point','A semicolon','A question mark','“Dear Senator Wilson:”'),q('Which phrase is more concise?','“because”','“due to the fact that”','“for the reason that”','“on account of the fact that”','Plain language.'),q('Should formal policy letters use contractions?','Generally avoid them','Always use them','Only with slang','Only in the subject line','Formal tone.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would proofread your letter in the final minutes of the event, naming common grammar, spelling and punctuation errors to check and how tone affects persuasion.',rubric:[['Scoring and time',['5 points','zero errors','eight minutes','proofread','spell check']],['Grammar',['subject-verb','fragment','run-on','comma splice','tense','its','their','affect']],['Spelling and punctuation',['constituent','legislature','commas','apostrophes','colon','proper nouns']],['Tone',['formal','respectful','concise','active voice','no slang','contractions']]],model:'I would stop drafting with at least eight minutes left, because grammar and spelling-punctuation are each worth 5 points only if the letter has zero errors, and even three or four errors drop each score to 3. First, I would run spell check and grammar check, but I would not rely on them, since they miss confused words. Then I would read the letter slowly, sentence by sentence from the end to the beginning, checking subject–verb agreement (“the bill provides”), sentence fragments that lack a main clause, run-ons and comma splices such as “The bill is vital, it helps students,” consistent verb tense and confused words like its and it’s, their, there and they’re, affect and effect, and then and than. For spelling, I would check common policy words such as constituent, legislature, appropriation, committee, necessary and sincerely, plus every proper noun—the official’s name, the bill number and title, my school and my chartered association. For punctuation, I would confirm commas after introductory phrases and before conjunctions joining two clauses, apostrophes for possession, a colon after “Dear Senator Wilson” and no exclamation points. Finally, I would check tone: formal and respectful, with no slang, contractions or sarcasm, concise phrases like “because” instead of “due to the fact that” and active voice. A clean, professional letter makes a policymaker take the writer more seriously, so careful proofreading strengthens persuasion as well as the score.'},sources:[RULES,OWL_COMMA,PLAIN]};

export const EW10={title:'Test-day strategy and a complete model letter',hook:'On competition day, you will walk in with a charged device, a memorized template and a plan for every minute. Everything else—the topic, the official, the bill—will be new. This final lesson puts the whole process together with a test-day checklist and a complete model letter for the HOSA sample topic.',objectives:['Prepare your device and materials for competition day.','Follow a minute-by-minute plan for the 60-minute writing period and 5-minute submission.','Use a memorized template to secure format points quickly.','Evaluate a complete model letter against the rubric.'],sections:[
['Before the event',[
'**Device**: bring a personal laptop or tablet (school devices may block uploads) that is **fully charged**—electricity is not provided—with a typing program you know well, **internet access** and a **USB port**. Turn off notifications, close other programs and set your document font to a clear, standard style.',
'**Upload link**: HOSA emails each ILC competitor a **unique digital upload link** after registration and again about five days before the competition. **Save the email** where you can open it quickly. Event staff have a backup list if needed. At regional and state conferences, the submission process may differ (USB, online form), so ask your advisor.',
'**Preparation**: practice at least five timed letters on different topics, memorize your template and evidence bank, wear business attire for bonus points and bring your photo ID. Remember: no printed materials or notes are allowed.',
order('Competition-day checklist',['Device fully charged with typing program, internet and USB','Notifications off and other apps closed','Upload link email saved and easy to find','Photo ID and business attire','Template and evidence bank memorized','Arrive early and calm'])]],
['A minute-by-minute plan',[
'**0–5 minutes**: read the topic twice and type your memorized format—constituent address and email, date, addressee name and address, subject line, salutation, closing and HOSA identification. **5–10**: choose your position and outline three distinct points, a story and a rebuttal. **10–40**: draft the opening, three body paragraphs and personal story. At the **30-minute announcement**, you should be finishing your second point.',
'**40–50**: write the conclusion and request for a response, then revise for clarity and concrete detail. **50–58**: proofread for grammar, spelling and punctuation and check all eight required items. **58–60**: delete any notes, save and prepare to upload. At the **5- and 1-minute announcements**, make only small fixes. Then use the **five extra minutes** to upload through your link.',
'Practice this plan until it is natural. If you fall behind, shorten body paragraphs rather than skipping the conclusion or proofreading.',
flip('Minute-by-minute plan',[['0–5','Read twice; type format'],['5–10','Position and outline'],['10–40','Draft opening, points and story'],['40–50','Conclusion, request and revision'],['50–58','Proofread and check required items'],['After 60','Five minutes to upload']])]],
['The memorized template',[
'Memorize a template you can type in under five minutes: **[Your name] / [Street address] / [City, State ZIP] / [Email]**; **[Date]**; **[Addressee name and title] / [Address from topic]**; **Subject: [Support for/Opposition to] [Bill number and name]**; **Dear [Title] [Last name]:**; body paragraphs; **Sincerely, / [Your name]**; **[Your name], [Division], HOSA Chapter #[number], [School], [Chartered Association]**.',
'Use a **realistic address** in the state named in the topic. Your constituent details may be invented for the event (do not share private information you are uncomfortable with), but they must be complete. Check that your subject line matches your final position.',
'Typing the template first secures **15+ format points** early and gives your letter a professional frame.',
chk('Why type the template first?',['It secures all-or-nothing format points early and frames the letter','It wastes time','Judges do not score format','It replaces the body'],'Format items are easy points.')]],
['A complete model letter (part 1)',[
ex('Model letter: format and opening (invented details)','HOSA sample topic: support HB-300.',['Jordan Rivera / 45 Elm Street / Cricket, MT 55555 / jordan.rivera@email.com','[Date]','Senator Marie Wilson / PO Box 1234 / Cricket, MT 55555','Subject: Support for HB-300, Funding for Career and Technology Student Organizations','Dear Senator Wilson:','When I joined HOSA as a freshman, I had never taken a blood pressure reading; this year, I helped teach hands-only CPR to 60 members of our community. As a Montana high school student and your constituent, I urge you to vote yes on HB-300, which would provide $500,000 to seven Career and Technology Student Organizations, including HOSA. These organizations turn classroom learning into real skills, and HB-300 would strengthen Montana’s health workforce, expand student service in our communities and deliver strong value for a modest investment.'],'The format items and a clear, attention-catching statement of position are complete (example invented for practice).'),
'Notice how the opening names the bill, the amount and the requested action, and how its last sentence previews three distinct points—workforce, community service and value.']],
['A complete model letter (part 2)',[
ex('Model letter: body, story and conclusion (invented details)','Continue the HB-300 letter.',['Point 1 (workforce): “First, HB-300 would help address Montana’s shortage of health professionals. Through HOSA, students explore careers, earn certifications such as CPR and compete in skills like nursing assisting and emergency medical technician, and many choose health majors as a result.”','Point 2 (community): “In addition, CTSOs serve our communities. Our chapter held a health fair at the library where volunteers screened residents’ blood pressure and taught hands-only CPR—services many rural Montanans rarely receive.”','Point 3 (value and rebuttal): “Most importantly, HB-300 is a wise use of public funds. While some question CTSOs’ effectiveness, I have seen classmates earn certifications and leadership roles; $500,000 shared among seven organizations reaches thousands of students.”','Personal story: “At that health fair, an older man’s reading was so high that the nurse sent him to a doctor that day; he had not had a checkup in years because the nearest clinic was an hour away. HOSA showed me how students can change lives.”','Conclusion and request: “For these reasons, HB-300 is an investment in the students who will care for Montana tomorrow. I would appreciate a reply letting me know your position. Thank you for your service.”','Close: “Sincerely, / Jordan Rivera / Jordan Rivera, Secondary Division, HOSA Chapter #000, Cricket High School, Montana HOSA”'],'Three distinct points with concrete detail, a rebuttal, a personal story with community significance, a fresh restatement and an explicit request for a response (example invented for practice).'),
'Evaluate the model against the rubric: Are all format items present? Is each point specific and concrete? Does the story show significance? Is the request explicit? Then write your own version on a different topic.']],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Falling behind',{a:{text:'At the 30-minute announcement you have only finished your opening. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Write shorter body paragraphs so you still have time for the conclusion and proofreading','b','Correct.',true],['Keep polishing the opening','b','Finish the letter first.',false]]},b:{text:'At the 5-minute warning, you notice your subject line says “Support” but you have argued in opposition. What do you do?',choices:[['Fix the subject line to match your position','c','Correct. Consistency matters.',true],['Leave it—judges will not notice','c','It would confuse the reader.',false]]},c:{text:'You save, then use the five extra minutes to upload with your unique link and confirm the submission.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Bring a fully charged personal device with a familiar typing program, internet and USB; save your unique HOSA upload email; no notes or printed materials; dress professionally and practice at least five timed letters.','Plan the hour: 0–5 read and type the template, 5–10 outline, 10–40 draft, 40–50 conclude and revise, 50–58 proofread and check all eight items, 58–60 save; then five minutes to upload; shorten paragraphs if behind.','A memorized template secures format points; the model letter shows a clear opening, three distinct concrete points with a rebuttal, a personal story with community significance, a fresh conclusion, an explicit request for a response and complete HOSA identification.'],terms:[['Template','A memorized letter structure typed at the start of the event.'],['Unique upload link','The personal HOSA Digital Upload link emailed to each ILC competitor.'],['Revision','Improving clarity, detail and organization after drafting.']],mcqs:[q('Why are personal devices encouraged?','School firewalls may block uploads','They are faster at typing','Judges prefer them','They have more games','Avoid barriers.'),q('How do ILC competitors submit their letters?','Through a unique HOSA Digital Upload link','By printing them','By email to judges','On paper','Saved email link.'),q('What should you do in the first five minutes?','Read the topic and type the format','Proofread','Upload','Write the conclusion','Secure format points.'),q('Where should you be at the 30-minute announcement?','Finishing your second supporting point','Still reading the topic','Uploading','Starting the format','Stay on schedule.'),q('If you fall behind, you should:','Shorten body paragraphs to save time for the conclusion and proofreading','Skip the conclusion','Skip proofreading','Stop writing','Finish the letter.'),q('How much time is given to upload after writing?','Five minutes','Thirty minutes','No extra time','One hour','Submission window.'),q('Which must match your final position?','The subject line','Your school name','The date','The addressee','Consistency.'),q('What is NOT allowed at the event?','Printed notes','A charged laptop','A typing program','A USB port','Memorize instead.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe your complete test-day strategy for Extemporaneous Writing – Health Policy, from preparing your device to submitting your letter, and explain how it protects each section of the rubric.',rubric:[['Preparation',['charged','personal device','typing program','internet','usb','upload link','no notes']],['Time plan',['0-5','format','outline','draft','conclusion','proofread','58','five minutes','upload']],['Rubric protection',['format points','content','three points','story','request','grammar','spelling']],['Adjustments',['behind','shorten','subject line','consistent','save']]],model:'Before competition day, I would practice at least five timed letters, memorize my template and evidence bank and charge my personal laptop fully, since electricity is not provided and school devices may block the upload site. I would check that my typing program, internet connection and USB port work, turn off notifications, save the email with my unique HOSA upload link and bring my photo ID in business attire. When the timer starts, I would spend the first five minutes reading the topic twice and typing my memorized format—constituent address and email, date, the addressee’s exact name and address, a subject line, a titled salutation, my closing and my HOSA identifying line—which secures the format points early. From minutes 5 to 10, I would choose the position I can argue best and outline three distinct points, a personal story and a rebuttal, protecting the 75 content points. I would draft from minutes 10 to 40, using the 30-minute announcement to check that I am on my second point. From 40 to 50, I would write a conclusion that restates my position in a new way and an explicit request for a response, protecting those 15 points, then revise for concrete detail. From 50 to 58, I would proofread for grammar, spelling and punctuation and confirm all eight required items, protecting the final 10 points. If I fell behind, I would shorten body paragraphs rather than skip the conclusion or proofreading. I would make sure my subject line matches my position, delete my notes, save and use the five extra minutes to upload through my link.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOADS,OWL_LETTER]};
